# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- Range#clamp (Ruby 4.1) ---
#
# Issue #1691. Ruby 4.1 adds `Range#clamp` (`range.c`, call-seq
# `clamp(min, max) -> range` / `clamp(range) -> range`), which returns a new
# range whose ends are clamped to the bounds. No rbs release through 4.2
# declares it, so `(1..10).clamp(2, 5)` reported `call.undefined-method` on
# correct 4.1 code.
#
# Either bound may be `nil` (unbounded), which leaves that end of the
# receiver as it is. The first two overloads answer the common case, bounds
# of the receiver's own element type, as `Range[Elem]`. Bounds of another
# type (`(1..10).clamp(2.5, nil)`) take the `untyped` arms rather than be
# reported. Those arms are deliberately not generic: a `[T] (T min, T max)`
# arm binds `T` to `Float | nil` for a `Float?` bound and puts a `nil` in the
# result's elements that CRuby never produces, which then reports
# `call.possible-nil-receiver` on correct code that iterates the result.
#
# TEMPORARY: delete this file once the rbs release Rigor bundles declares
# the method upstream.
#
# Declared unconditionally, not gated on `target_ruby` (ADR-121 § Rejected
# alternatives; false positives outrank), and on a module `Range` includes
# so that a later direct declaration overrides it instead of raising
# `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` for the whole class (see `process.rbs`).
# `Elem` is `unchecked` on the module because the first two overloads read it
# in parameter position.
module RigorCoreOverlay
  module Ruby41Range[unchecked out Elem]
    def clamp: (Elem? min, Elem? max) -> Range[Elem]
             | (Range[Elem?] range) -> Range[Elem]
             | (untyped min, untyped max) -> Range[untyped]
             | (Range[untyped] range) -> Range[untyped]
  end
end

class Range[out Elem]
  include RigorCoreOverlay::Ruby41Range[Elem]
end
